  "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =({x}^{2}-4x+1)\\cdot {e}^{x}+(2x-4){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2x-3)\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(x+1)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(x+1)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-1$ and $x=3$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(-2) & ={e}^{-2}(-1)(-5)=5{e}^{-2}>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(0) & ={e}^{0}(1)(-3)=-3<0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(4) & ={e}^{4}(5)(1)=5{e}^{4}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{\u2032}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\u2197$ | max | $\u2198$ | min | $\u2197$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=3,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=3$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=3.$",
